::SPEECH_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
A classmate asks what to submit for the checkpoint. Which response uses the clearest instructional communication?
{
=A. Submit a short response that explains the situation, audience, and respectful wording you would use.#Correct. This response names the assignment and gives clear details about what must be included.
~B. Just submit the thing from the page.#This is too vague. Instructional communication should name the task and explain what the student needs to do.
~C. I do not know, so do whatever you want.#This does not help the classmate understand the direction. A useful response should clarify or suggest how to find the answer.
~D. You should already know the directions.#This blames the classmate and gives no instructional support. A respectful response helps the next step happen.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which question best asks a Teacher of Record for clarification?
{
~A. This assignment makes no sense.#This shows frustration but does not ask a specific question. Clarification should name what is unclear.
~B. Can you help me with everything?#This is too broad. A better question identifies the exact part that needs help.
=C. I understand Part A, but could you clarify what Part B should include?#Correct. This question names what is understood and asks about the specific unclear part.
~D. Why is this so hard?#This does not guide the Teacher of Record toward the support needed. A clear question focuses on the assignment.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best supports collaboration during a group task?
{
~A. I will do my part and ignore the group.#This does not support collaboration. Group communication requires shared understanding and participation.
=B. Let's review the directions together and decide who will complete each part.#Correct. This response supports the group, uses the directions, and creates a clear next step.
~C. The loudest person should choose the plan.#This is not collaborative. Good discussion allows group members to contribute respectfully.
~D. We should stop talking about the assignment.#This avoids the task instead of helping the group understand what to do.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which response uses academic language to explain a school task?
{
~A. Do the stuff from the thing.#This is vague and does not use academic language. The listener needs exact words about the task.
~B. Just make it look finished.#This focuses on appearance instead of the learning expectation. Academic communication should explain the task clearly.
~C. Put words there until it seems okay.#This is unclear and unhelpful. The student needs precise directions.
=D. Write one claim and one reason that supports your communication choice.#Correct. This response uses academic words and explains the expected work clearly.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
A group member says, "I do not know what I should do next." Which reply is best?
{
~A. That is your problem.#This is disrespectful and does not help the group move forward.
=B. Let's check the directions and identify the next step for your part.#Correct. This is respectful, specific, and helpful because it uses the directions to support the next action.
~C. I guess you can sit there.#This does not support the classmate or the group task. Social and instructional communication should help participation.
~D. You should have listened earlier.#This blames the group member instead of solving the communication problem.
}
